#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Assemble independent lecture packages from audited material and a new door lab.
Does not generate audio, render films, change the combined reel, or publish.
"""
from pathlib import Path
import ast,json,shutil,subprocess,hashlib,re,copy
S=Path(__file__).resolve().parent;Y=S.parent;O=Y/'claude-liam-lecture-scenes-collision-physics';L=S/'evidence-lab';T=Y.parent.parent/'brutalist.art'
films=json.loads((S/'films.json').read_text());old=json.loads((O/'content.json').read_text());rows={b['id']:b for b in old['body']}
def new(id,topic,lane,asset,narration,motion,why,runner='A text summary would hide the mechanism'):
 rows[id]={'id':id,'topic':topic,'lane':lane,'asset':asset,'narration':narration,'motion':motion,'why':why,'runner_up':runner,'source':'New authored teaching beat; exact code and evidence sources in excerpt-map and FACTCHECK.'}
new('BS01','A scene is not necessarily a level','capture','docs-nodes','Hallo is our greeting; composition is our subject. A scene can be a door, a character, or a whole level. Nodes supply capabilities. Saving their arrangement lets us reuse the object without rebuilding every part.','Move from the official scene definition to its node illustration','Official engine documentation grounds the term before a custom example','A conceptual node diagram is useful after the actual definition')
# Keep the body in an instructional register; the greeting belongs only to BIDEA.
rows['BS01']['narration']='A scene can be a door, a character, or a whole level. Nodes supply capabilities. Saving their arrangement lets us reuse the object without rebuilding every part. Think of the scene as a reusable object boundary, not a synonym for level.'
new('BS02','Read the actual door hierarchy','code','door-tree','Our door combines a visible panel, a solid body, and an approach area. Each collision shape belongs directly to the object it describes. This tree is derived from the saved door scene; the door owns the behavior of the assembly.','Reveal the root, visual children, solid branch and sensor branch in that order','The real saved hierarchy answers where each capability belongs','A generic organization chart would omit the actual node types')
new('BS03','Export a setting, preserve the boundary','code','door-exports','The locked property is exported so each door instance can start with a different setting. Is open is runtime state. Both belong to the door script. The level can configure the public setting without reaching through the scene to manipulate private children.','Reveal exported configuration before runtime fields','Executed script exposes the configuration/state distinction directly','An Inspector mockup would not be actual engine evidence')
new('BS04','Connect at the composition boundary','code','door-wiring','The level creates two doors and connects the first door\'s approach signal. The door reports an actor; the level decides what that means for the level. No hard-coded chain of parent lookups is needed to find the recipient.','Reveal instantiation, exported override and parent signal connection','Exact executed fixture demonstrates real signal ownership','A conceptual signal diagram is already used for the coin')
new('BS05','State has a lifetime','code','door-open','A fresh door starts closed. Opening one instance changes that instance\'s state and shape; it does not open its neighbor. If a door must remain unlocked across level changes, store that progression outside the disposable instance and restore it deliberately.','Reveal the state guard, state update and deferred shape change','Executed door state is a concrete example; persistence is explicitly a design extension','A global-state diagram would introduce an unimplemented save system')
new('BT01','Collider and trigger are roles','manim','BlockOrDetect','People often use collider to mean a solid boundary, and trigger to mean an overlap sensor. Both use collision geometry. In Godot, the owning physics body or area determines the role. The same rectangle can stop a character or report its passage.','Move matched actors toward equal rectangles; stop one, pass the other, mark an entry event','A spatial comparison makes the behavioral difference visible before code','A static table cannot demonstrate blocking versus passage')
new('BT02','Geometry does not choose the role','code','door-tree','Collision Shape Two D supplies geometry. Put it under a physics body to participate in that body\'s collision behavior. Put it under Area Two D to define a sensor region. The three D equivalents follow the same separation.','Reveal each owner before its shape child','The actual door tree shows both ownership arrangements together','A screenshot of unrelated Inspector settings would obscure ownership')
new('BT03','Predict the controlled comparison','capture','comparison-before','Here is an actual scripted Godot experiment. Two identical character shapes receive the same movement requests. The upper rectangle belongs to a static body. The lower one belongs to an area. Predict which character stops, and which produces overlap events.','Establish both rows, then focus on each equal-sized rectangle','The engine output anchors the prediction in a runnable setup','Conceptual motion is reserved for the preceding mechanism')
new('BT04','Read the observed result','capture','comparison-after','The upper character stopped at the solid boundary. The lower character crossed the area, producing one entry and one exit. The geometry was the same size. This result tests this setup; it is not a guarantee about every speed or shape.','Move focus from the stopped character to the passed character and event counters','Actual captured outcome closes the prediction loop','A shell count alone would hide spatial behavior')
new('BT05','A sensor scans a category','code','sensor-mask','The player lives on layer three, whose bit value is four. The door sensor has no membership layer in this example, but its mask includes the player. A sensor can detect a target without the target scanning the sensor in return.','Reveal the player layer and the approach mask from the executed source','Real settings connect the directed query to named layer numbers','A matrix is useful for larger policies, but this is one directed query')
new('BT06','Monitoring and monitorable differ','shell','sensor-controls','Monitoring asks whether this area detects overlaps. Monitorable asks whether other monitoring areas can detect this area. Our tests disable each independently. For the monitorable test, we move the target out and back before checking the new overlap.','Reveal the monitoring test results, then the separate monitorable target results','Recorded tests distinguish the observer setting from the target setting','An untested property list would invite the common reversal')
new('BT07','Choose the event for the target','code','door-events','Body entered and body exited report physics bodies. Area entered and area exited report other areas. Our door listens for bodies because the player is a character body. A weapon area hitting a hurtbox area needs the area signals instead.','Reveal the body signal connections and their handlers','The real door script explains the selected API without inventing a weapon implementation','A broad signal catalog would distract from the target type')
new('BT08','Overlap results belong to physics updates','manim','OverlapClock','Overlap lists are updated during physics processing. Moving an object and immediately reading a cached list can show the previous result. Prefer enter and exit signals for transitions. In an automated experiment, wait for the relevant physics steps before asserting the outcome.','Move an actor into the region, retain the old cached count, then advance a tick and update it','A labeled sequence makes timing and stale reads explicit','A static property reference cannot show temporal ordering')
new('BT09','Turn transitions into occupancy','code','door-occupancy','An entry is an event. Occupancy is state. Store each entering actor by instance identifier and erase it on exit. Weak references let this door discard actors that were freed. A dictionary also avoids mistaking one actor\'s exit for an empty room.','Reveal insertion, erasure and stale-reference cleanup','Executed door logic connects edge events to persistent occupancy','A single boolean demonstration would hide multiple-actor limitations')
new('BT10','Continuous effects need a policy','manim','Occupancy','There is no body stay signal in this interface. For a damage zone, enter and exit can maintain a target set, while a timer applies damage at a chosen interval. Decide whether damage happens immediately on entry and how cooldowns work.','Mark one enter, several deliberate timer pulses while occupied, then exit stops pulses','A constructed timeline distinguishes event frequency from game policy','A code listing would imply a tested damage system we have not built')
new('BT11','A door needs both roles','capture','door-closed','This door has a narrow solid panel inside a larger approach sensor. The sensor can notice the player before the player reaches the panel. Detection requests an interaction; it does not automatically authorize opening. The locked setting remains a separate gameplay rule.','Focus on the outer approach region, then the narrow blocking panel','Actual engine capture shows both regions in one reusable scene','A conceptual door illustration would lose the tested geometry')
new('BT12','Open the panel, keep the sensor','code','door-open','The open method first checks the lock and the open-state guard. It then disables only the solid shape, using a deferred change. The approach area remains active. Removing the entire door would also remove the sensor and its occupancy information.','Reveal guards before the deferred shape mutation','Executed implementation shows exactly what changes','A before-and-after picture alone hides the deferred mutation')
new('BT13','Close only when the region is clear','shell','door-checks','The test verifies that a locked door refuses opening, an open door keeps its sensor, and an occupied door refuses closure. After the actor exits, closing restores the solid shape. Multiple actors arriving during closure still deserve a separate stress test.','Reveal lock, open sensor, occupied closure and exit restoration checks','Actual assertions establish scope and leave concurrency limits visible','A door animation would not establish the guards')
new('BT14','Nonblocking does not mean powerless','capture','coin','An area can also influence local gravity or damping; it still does not act as a solid wall. A rigid body can report contacts too when configured. Choose geometry, physical response, event reporting, and gameplay state as separate decisions.','Focus on the real nonblocking pickup region and its detected actor','The tested pickup grounds the role; API extensions are identified in narration','A fabricated gravity demonstration would overstate the available evidence')
new('BP01','Force and impulse have different clocks','manim','ForceImpulse','A force acts over time. An impulse represents a one-time momentum change. With the same impulse, doubling mass halves the ideal velocity change. Do not repeatedly apply a jump impulse every rendered frame; that changes the intended event into repeated pushes.','Draw force over an interval and one impulse marker, then compare velocity arrows for two masses','A labeled ideal model separates the continuous push from a discrete event','A numerical trace without controlled damping would obscure the distinction')
new('BP02','Contact reporting is opt-in','code','contact-monitor','A rigid body can collide even when your script receives no contact signal. For body entered reporting, enable contact monitoring and allow enough reported contacts. Our crate fixture sets both and observes a floor contact. Reporting and physical response are separate concerns.','Reveal contact_monitor and max_contacts_reported before the connected callback','The executed fixture explains a silent callback without pretending collision failed','A conceptual warning card would not show the required settings')
layouts=[
[['BS01','B03','B04','BS02'],['B05','BS03','B18','B06'],['B07','BS04','B08','BS05'],['B09','B02','B53','B52','B54']],
[['B14','B10','B11','B12'],['B15','B16','B17','B18','BT11'],['B32','B33','B34','B35','B36'],['B38','B39','B43','B47','B48','B50']],
[['BT01','BT02','BT03','BT04'],['BT05','B17','BT06','BT07','BT08'],['B28','B29','BT09','BT10','B30','B31'],['BT11','BT12','BT13','BT14']],
[['B01','B19','B10','B12'],['B20','B21','B22','B23','B24','B25','B27'],['B11','BP01','BP02','B26','B49','B51'],['B37','B38','B39','B40','B41','B42','B44','B45','B46','B46A']]
]
bookends=[
 dict(summary='Build reusable objects with clear scene boundaries.',description='Build reusable scene boundaries',title_lines=['Scenes and Instances','in Godot'],framing='A scene is one level',trigger='one level',replacement='a reusable object',intro='Hallo. This is Liam, in for Bear. If scene means level to you, Godot\'s structure seems larger than it is. Start with a door: one reusable object whose parts have clear jobs.',terms=[['Node','One capability in a tree.'],['Tree','A parent-child arrangement.'],['Instance','A live copy of a definition.'],['Resource','Data that nodes can share.']],recap=['A scene packages nodes into a reusable object.','Instance settings and shared resources have different ownership.','Signals keep communication at explicit object boundaries.','A small fixture makes composition testable.'],prompt='Help me build a reusable Godot four door scene with a visible panel, a solid body, and an approach area. Export a locked setting. Instantiate two doors with different settings, connect an approach signal in the level, and test that opening one leaves the other closed. State which resources are shared and which state must persist outside a scene.',checks=['Check: two instances have independent state and explicit resource ownership.','Check: the door reports an event without a hard-coded parent path.']),
 dict(summary='Choose boundaries and filtering that match the intended interaction.',description='Define who can collide with whom',title_lines=['Collision Shapes, Layers,','and Masks in Godot'],framing='The sprite is the collision',trigger='the collision',replacement='only the appearance',intro='Hallo. This is Liam, in for Bear. A visible wall can have the wrong boundary, and a correctly drawn pickup can scan the wrong category. We will separate geometry, body roles, and filtering so each failure has a test.',terms=[['Shape','Geometry used for contact.'],['Body','A collision object with a role.'],['Layer','A membership category.'],['Mask','Categories the object scans.']],recap=['Choose geometry and body roles independently of artwork.','Named layers and masks express an interaction policy.','Tiles and one-way surfaces need deliberate contact rules.','Diagnose a failed boundary with a controlled test.'],prompt='Help me build a Godot four collision test room. Include a character, a solid floor, a one-way platform, a pickup area, and rigid debris. Give every object named layers and masks in an interaction matrix. Predict the result of clearing the pickup mask and test it. Explain how you would identify a struck tile cell and verify drop-through clearance.',checks=['Check: the matrix explains at least one deliberate exclusion.','Check: the mask experiment matches the prediction; untested cases are listed.']),
 dict(summary='Separate solid contact, overlap sensing, and gameplay decisions.',description='Build a door that blocks and detects',title_lines=['Triggers and Colliders','in Godot'],framing='A trigger is a broken collider',trigger='a broken collider',replacement='an overlap sensor',intro='Hallo. This is Liam, in for Bear. Walking through a trigger does not mean collision is broken. It means the region detects you without being a wall. A door shows why one object often needs both roles.',terms=[['Shape','The boundary geometry.'],['Body','An object with physical response.'],['Area','A region for overlap detection.'],['Signal','An event another object can handle.']],recap=['A solid boundary blocks while a trigger detects overlap.','Masks and monitoring settings determine sensor detection.','Transition events need explicit gameplay state and timing.','A door combines a solid panel with an independent sensor.'],prompt='Help me build a Godot four door with a solid panel and a larger approach area. Use the same-shaped body and area experiment to demonstrate blocking versus detection. Keep the sensor active while opening, defer physics changes, and track occupancy so an occupied door refuses closure. Test locked, open, occupied, and exited states. Distinguish monitoring from monitorable.',checks=['Check: the actor crosses the area but stops at the solid boundary.','Check: opening disables only the panel; occupancy prevents closure.']),
 dict(summary='Design motion, let the solver work, and measure the result.',description='Design motion and test its limits',title_lines=['Character Motion and','Rigid-Body Physics','in Godot'],framing='The physics engine controls everything',trigger='controls everything',replacement='shares responsibility with code',intro='Hallo. This is Liam, in for Bear. A character jump is a movement policy you design. A falling crate asks the solver to handle motion. Mixing those responsibilities makes both harder to tune. We will choose an owner for every motion.',terms=[['Velocity','Movement rate and direction.'],['Acceleration','Change in velocity over time.'],['Tick','One physics update step.'],['Impulse','A one-time momentum change.']],recap=['Choose whether code or the solver owns the motion.','A character jump starts with design values and measured behavior.','Rigid simulation needs deliberate forces and contact reporting.','Fixed ticks, presentation, and numerical evidence answer different questions.'],prompt='Help me build a Godot four physics test room with a character jump, a falling rigid crate, and an animatable platform. Choose a jump height and apex time, derive the upward acceleration, then measure the resulting height within a declared tolerance. Use physics callbacks correctly, compare an impulse with a sustained force, and list platform, interpolation, and device checks still requiring a playtest.',checks=['Check: the measured jump is compared with a declared target and tolerance.','Check: code and solver ownership are explicit; untested behavior is named.'])]
# Every original body topic has a destination. B13 is incorporated more deeply by new trigger beats.
covered={id for acts in layouts for group in acts for id in group};missing=set(b['id'] for b in old['body'])-covered
assert missing=={'B13'},missing
